WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.880
Ronaldo, Messi and Zlatan are warming up

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.900
before an ALL STARS football game

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:08.920
Ronaldo gets the ball, and passes it to Zlatan

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.200
Zlatan then passes the ball to Messi…

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.500
And then Messi passes the ball back to Zlatan

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.100
and many such passes are exchanged.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.900
Now it was pretty easy for me to explain this to you diagrammatically.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:23.280
But what if I wanted to explain this to a COMPUTER?

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:25.580
The computer will not understand diagrams.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:30.480
So we make a small table on the right with 3 rows and 3 columns.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.660
We can have Ronaldo, Messi and Zlatan as rows.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.820
And to know ‘who the ball was passed to’,

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:39.420
we have Ronaldo, Messi and Zlatan as columns.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:42.000
We all know what Zlatan is capable of

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.820
but let’s assume the players can’t pass the ball to themselves.

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:48.320
So these three elements will be zero.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:51.560
There is ONE arrow going from R towards M.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:53.300
So we write a one here.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.760
And there’s just one pass from Ronaldo to Zlatan.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.100
Similarly we fill the rest of the slots.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.880
What we see here is nothing but a MATRIX.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.140
It is nothing but an ARRAY of elements…

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.940
something which a computer can decode!

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:10.120
And it summarizes the entire warm up session.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:14.320
Adding up all the numbers here will give us the total number of passes.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.140
And it also tells us the DIRECTION of the passes!

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:22.620
Don’t you wish matrices were taught like this in school?

